.TH BPFRAG 8 "2026-10" "bpfrag 0.6.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bpfrag \- eBPF network tuning for competitive gaming that undoes itself
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bpfrag
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B bpfrag
.BR \-\-check " | " \-\-selftest " | " \-\-restore " | " \-\-dump\-config " | " \-\-report
.br
.B bpfrag \-\-classify
.I pid
.br
.B bpfrag
.BR \-\-monitor " \fIsecs\fR | " \-\-waybar
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bpfrag
counts UDP traffic per process with eBPF. When a game's network traffic
appears (a steady, two\-way stream of small packets from a process that is
a game), it applies a networking profile chosen from the tick rate:
.BR CASUAL ,
.B COMPETITIVE
or
.BR INTENSE .
Depending on the tier, that covers socket busy\-polling, NIC interrupt
coalescing, socket buffer headroom, DSCP marking of the game's packets,
Wi\-Fi power save and, optionally, tighter dirty\-page writeback.
.PP
Each original value is written to a journal before it is changed. When
the match ends or the game exits, every change is put back. If the daemon
is killed,
.B bpfrag \-\-restore
(run by the systemd unit's
.BR ExecStopPost )
puts back whatever the journal records.
.PP
.B bpfrag
never changes CPU scheduling, so it runs next to sched_ext schedulers.
While
.BR bpftune (8)
is running, bpfrag leaves the sysctls bpftune tunes alone.
.PP
Each match gets a health report, shown by
.B \-\-monitor
and logged when it ends: the gaps between the game's incoming packets
(timed at tc ingress), how much the kernel's receive drop
counters grew, and which receive queue and IRQ the game's packets
arrive on.
.B \-\-dry\-run
measures the same way, which gives an untuned baseline.
.PP
With
.B [bulk]
enabled in the configuration file, other processes moving bulk data during
a match (a download, a torrent client) have their packets marked with DSCP
LE, in both directions, so CAKE or a QoS router serves the game first.
Streaming hosts, voice apps and VPN clients are never demoted, packets
already marked are left alone, and nothing is marked outside a match or in
a dry run.
.PP
With
.B [experiment]
set, one setting (for example
.BR busy_polling )
is switched off in a random share of matches, so its effect can be
measured. Each match logs which it got, and its health line ends with it.
With
.BR "setting = \(dqrandom\(dq" ,
bpfrag picks the setting itself, once a day (from 05:00) or once a boot,
from a pool for wired or Wi\-Fi connections, leaving out settings the
machine already has; the pick is kept in
.IR /var/lib/bpfrag .
.PP
On the system bus,
.B bpfrag
publishes a read\-only interface,
.BR com.performativenonsense.Bpfrag1 :
properties describing the match in progress and signals when one starts,
changes tier and ends. Any user can read it; nothing on the bus can change
what bpfrag does. Set
.B dbus = false
under
.B [general]
to turn it off.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIfile\fR"
TOML file with tiers, classifier lists and detector settings. Defaults to
.I /etc/bpfrag/bpfrag.toml
if it exists.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Detect and log what would change, but change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tier " \fItier\fR"
Use this tier for every match instead of following the tick rate:
.BR casual ", " competitive " or " intense .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dscp " \fIclass\fR"
DSCP class for game packets
.RB ( CS4 ", " EF ", " AF41 ,
or a number 0\-63), or
.B off
to disable marking.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-iface " \fIiface\fR"
Interface to tune and mark on. Repeatable. Default: the interfaces that
carry the default routes.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-allow " \fIglob\fR"
Process name glob that is always a game. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-deny " \fIglob\fR"
Process name glob that is never a game. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-min\-pps " \fIpps\fR"
Smoothed packets per second a process needs before it can count as a match.
.TP
.BR \-\-linger " \fIsecs\fR"
Seconds without game traffic before a match is considered over.
.TP
.BR \-\-bpftune " \fImode\fR"
How to share sysctls with bpftune:
.B auto
(yield while it runs, the default),
.B yield
(always) or
.BR ignore .
.TP
.BR \-\-stats " \fIsecs\fR"
Log a stats line every
.I secs
while running.
.TP
.B \-\-waybar
Print one line of JSON for a Waybar custom module and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-monitor " \fIsecs\fR"
Show the running instance's stats every
.I secs
(0 shows them once) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-restore
Undo anything a crashed or killed instance left changed, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report what this kernel and these interfaces support, every setting's
value now and what each tier would set it to, the kernel's tick rate and
preemption model, the default qdisc, TCP congestion control and
NetworkManager's Wi-Fi power save default, and which other tuners are
active, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-classify " \fIpid\fR"
Classify
.I pid
the way the daemon would and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-selftest
Push test packets through the DSCP marker and the arrival meter with
BPF_PROG_TEST_RUN and exit. Nothing is attached and nothing is changed.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-config
Print the effective configuration as TOML and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-report
Print a diagnostic report for a bug report, as Markdown, and exit: the
version, OS and kernel, the output of
.BR \-\-check ,
.BR \-\-selftest ,
.B \-\-monitor
and
.BR \-\-dump\-config ,
the service state, the restore journal and this boot's log. The hostname,
user names in home paths, and IP and MAC addresses are replaced. Run it as
root for the complete report.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More output. Give it twice to include libbpf's messages.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BPFRAG_GAME
Set in a game's environment (for example in Steam launch options:
.BR "BPFRAG_GAME=1 %command%" ).
A true value marks the process as a game; a false value, such as
.BR 0 ,
excludes it.
.TP
.B BPFRAG_OBSERVE
Set in a game's environment
.RB ( "BPFRAG_OBSERVE=1 %command%" ),
a true value has the UDP and TCP traffic of the process, and of every
process it starts, logged: message rates, sizes and how steady they are.
It changes nothing else; add
.B BPFRAG_GAME=0
to log a game without ever tuning for it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/bpfrag/bpfrag.toml
Configuration: tiers, allow and deny lists, detector settings.
.TP
.I /etc/default/bpfrag
Extra command line flags for the systemd service
.RB ( BPFRAG_FLAGS ).
.TP
.I /run/bpfrag/journal.json
Original values of everything changed during the current match.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus\-1/system.d/com.performativenonsense.Bpfrag1.conf
D\-Bus policy: only root may own the name, anyone may read it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on error.
.B \-\-check
exits 1 if bpfrag cannot run on this machine, and
.B \-\-selftest
exits 1 if any test vector fails.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the machine, then run in the foreground without changing anything:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo bpfrag \-\-check
sudo bpfrag \-\-dry\-run \-v
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run as a service:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo systemctl enable \-\-now bpfrag
journalctl \-u bpfrag \-f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Collect a report to attach to a bug report:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo bpfrag \-\-report > bpfrag\-report.md
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bpftune (8),
.BR busctl (1),
.BR ethtool (8),
.BR iw (8),
.BR tc\-bpf (8),
.BR sysctl (8)
